on, Sofia and Perugini, Marco and Peters, Kim and Pieters, Constant and Ponizovskiy, Vladimir and Porter, Nathaniel D and Purić, Danka and Purol, Mariah and Puthillam, Arathy and Quinn, Kimberly and Ramljak, Marco and Reed, W. Robert and Ritchie, Michaela and Ritzau, Margaret and Roche, Sean Patrick and Rodela, Romina and Röer, Jan Philipp and Ropovik, Ivan and Rothschild, Jacob and Saal, Justine and Safadi, Hani and Samaha, Jason and Sanchez, Mary and Sankaran, Soorya and Santos, David and Sargent, Amanda and Sauter, Marian and Schmidt, Kathleen and Schnabel, Landon and Schroeder, Amber and Schuetz, Sebastian W and {Schulte-Mecklenbeck}, Michael and Schütz, Astrid and Sevigny, Eric L. and Shackleton, Ellie and Shafranek, Richard and Shaki, Samuel and Shakya, Shishir and Sirota, Miroslav and Sisco, Matthew Ryan and Sitnikov, Maksim and Slevc, L. Robert and Smalarz, Laura and Smith, Colin and Snyder, Joel S. and Sommet, Nicolas and Sonmez, Fatih and Spellman, Bobbie and {Stanulewicz-Buckley}, Natalia and Stock, George and Street, Chris N. H. and Strømland, Eirik André and Sundelin, Tina and Syed, Moin and Szabelska, Anna and Szaszi, Barnabas and Szumowska, Ewa and Tagat, Anirudh and Täuber, Susanne and Tay, Louis and Thapa, Stuti and Thatcher, Jason and Tsaklakidou, Domna and Tummers, Lars and Turkovich, Elise and Tutor, Melba and Urbanska, Karolina and Van 'T Veer, Anna Elisabeth and Van Assen, Marcel A. L. M. and Van De Ven, Niels and Van Den Goorbergh, Ruben and Vargo, Elisabeth Julie and Vaughn, Leigh Ann and Vazire, Simine and Vermeulen, Jentien and Vo, Diem Thi Hong and Volkman, Victor and Wagenmakers, Eric-Jan and Wagner, Deliah and Walasek, Lukasz and Walter, Frank and Warmelink, Lara and Wei, Liuqing and Weißflog, Marie Isabelle and Weller, Nicholas and Wichman, Aaron Lee and Wilbiks, Jonathan and Williams, Jamal Rodgers and Wolfe, Kelly and Wort, Finnian and Wright, Ryan and Wulff, Jesper N. and Xue, Xindong and Yan, Veronica X. and Yang, Yuzhi and Yoon, Sangsuk and Zezelj, Iris and Zhang, Yinxian and Ziano, Ignazio and Zogmaister, Cristina and Zupan, Zorana and Zwaan, Rolf A. and Nosek, Brian A. and Errington, Timothy M.},
  year = 2026,
  month = apr,
  publisher = {MetaArXiv},
  doi = {10.31222/osf.io/b7x8f_v1},
  url = {https://osf.io/b7x8f_v1},
  urldate = {2026-05-04},
  abstract = {We attempted replications of 274 claims of positive results from 164 quantitative papers published from 2009 to 2018 in 54 journals in the social and behavioral sciences. Replications were high-powered on average to detect the original effect size (Median = 99.6\%), used original materials when relevant and available, and were peer-reviewed in advance through a standardized internal protocol. Replications showed statistically significant results in the original pattern for 151 of 274 claims (55.1\% [95\% CI 49.2 - 60.9\%]) and for 80.8 of 164 papers (49.3\% [95\% CI 43.8 - 54.7\%]) weighed for replicating multiple claims per paper. We observed modest variation in replication rates across disciplines (42.5\% to 63.1\%) though some estimates had high uncertainty. For claims with Pearson’s r effect sizes, the median was 0.25 [95\% CI 0.21 - 0.27] for original studies and 0.10 [95\% CI 0.09 - 0.13] for replication studies, a 82.4\% [95\% CI 67.8 - 88.2\%] reduction in shared variance. Thirteen methods for evaluating replication success provided estimates ranging from 28.6\% to 74.8\% (median = 49.3\%). Some decline in effect size and significance is expected based on power to detect original effects and regression to the mean due to replicating only positive results. The conditions that promote or inhibit replicability are worthy of additional investigation.},
